        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Add an exploding gradient detector.** Modify the `NetworkDebugger` to detect when gradients exceed a threshold and automatically suggest a gradient clipping value. Test it on a 20-layer network with no normalization.\n\n2. **Build a dead neuron resurrector.** Write a function that identifies dead ReLU neurons (always outputting 0) and reinitializes their incoming weights with Kaiming initialization. Show that this recovers a network where >70% of neurons are dead.\n\n3. **Implement the learning rate finder with plotting.** Extend `find_learning_rate` to save results as a CSV and write a separate script that reads the CSV and displays the LR vs loss curve using matplotlib. Identify the optimal LR for ResNet-18 on CIFAR-10.\n\n4. **Create a data pipeline validator.** Write a function that checks for: duplicate samples across train/test splits, label distribution imbalance (>10:1 ratio), input normalization (mean near 0, std near 1), and NaN/Inf values in the data. Run it on a deliberately corrupted dataset.\n\n5. **Debug a real failure.** Take the mini-framework from Lesson 10, introduce a subtle bug (e.g., transpose the weight matrix in backward), and use gradient checking to locate exactly which parameter has incorrect gradients. Document the debugging process."
      ]
    }
